    "result_text": "All 16 tests pass \u2014 **BUILD SUCCESS**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI changed telephone handling from the old 10-digit form to E.164 across three source files:\n\n**1. `ValidateOwnerFields.java`** \u2014 replaced `normalizeTelephone` (which stripped non-digits and required exactly 10) with an E.164 normalizer:\n- If the input starts with `+`, keep the `+` and its country code.\n- Otherwise assume `+61` and drop a single leading `0` from the national digits.\n- Strip spaces, dashes and brackets; reject if any non-digit remains.\n- Require 8\u201315 digits after the `+`, else add a `telephone` error (\u2192 400).\n- So `0412 345 678` \u2192 `+61412345678`, `+64 21 123 456` \u2192 `+6421123456`, `12` \u2192 rejected.\n\n**2. `CheckTelephoneUnique.java`** \u2014 duplicate detection now compares the stored E.164 strings directly (both request and stored owners are already E.164), so national and international forms of the same number collide (\u2192 409).\n\n**3. `Owner.java`** (entity) \u2014 relaxed the persistence-layer bean validation from `@Digits`/`@Pattern(\"^[0-9]{10}$\")` to `@Pattern(\"^\\\\+[0-9]{8,15}$\")` so the E.164 string with its leading `+` passes validation on save. Removed the now-unused `@Digits` import.\n\nThe DB `telephone` column is already `VARCHAR(20)`, which comfortably holds an E.164 value, and the generated request DTO is bound without `@Valid`, so its OpenAPI pattern has no runtime effect. All existing (Cp01\u2013Cp07) and new (Cp08) tests are green.",
